{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_rtu7vmlqqbfzmssowr2ss3ochnksrv24nmskv25wsk5cghbj4uma","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01VxXYfTSYZ7E1Pv2dfTxam6","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QUHmNR/translation/src/lib.rs","old_string":"//! * Accesses go through `read_unaligned` / `write_unaligned`. GCC compiles the\n//!   C's `*Red` to a bare `movss`, which has no alignment requirement, so the C\n//!   library happily transforms a `float*` that a caller aimed at an unaligned\n//!   offset. A plain `*red` in Rust promises 4-byte alignment instead, and a\n//!   debug build turns that promise into an abort (\"misaligned pointer\n//!   dereference\"), diverging from the C on an input the C accepts. The\n//!   unaligned accessors keep the exact same instruction for aligned pointers\n//!   and reproduce the C for unaligned ones. The read/write ORDER is unchanged:\n//!   all three components are read before any is written.","new_string":"//! * Every access goes through `sse::load` / `sse::store`, i.e. a literal\n//!   `movss`, which is what GCC emits for `*Red`. `movss` places no requirement\n//!   on the address, so the C library transforms a deliberately unaligned\n//!   `float*` and faults with `SIGSEGV` on a null one. A plain `*red` in Rust\n//!   promises alignment and non-nullness, and any build with\n//!   `-C debug-assertions` (every `cargo test` without `--release`) enforces\n//!   those promises by aborting with `SIGABRT` — a divergence from the C on\n//!   inputs the C accepts or fails differently on. Using the raw instruction\n//!   makes both Rust profiles behave exactly like the C. The read/write ORDER is\n//!   unchanged: all three components are read before any is written."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4922,"cache_read_input_