{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_elanaxrhp3j3iyeqwqcx4rfll25mri4u3xjndec2v3wvtvsh7bfq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four completion gates pass from a clean rebuild.\n\n## What the library is\n\nOne translation unit: `colourblind(cb_impairment, float*, float*, float*)` applying one of three colour-blindness matrices in place. Three `static` helpers, one exported symbol, no error channel.\n\n## Compile errors\n\nNone — `cargo check` was clean on first run. The `match impairment { cbProtanopia => ... }` arms resolve to const patterns (not bindings), confirmed by the absence of any unreachable-pattern warning.\n\n## Two real divergences found and fixed (in the Rust)\n\nBoth were exposed by the debug-profile tests, and both stem from the same root cause: **GCC compiles `*Red` to a bare `movss`, which constrains the address in no way**, whereas Rust's `*red` promises alignment and non-nullness and enforces those promises under `-C debug-assertions`.\n\n1. **Misaligned pointers** — C computes normally; Rust aborted with `misaligned pointer dereference`.\n2. **NULL pointers** — C dies with `SIGSEGV` (11); Rust died with `SIGABRT` (6).\n\nFix: accesses now go through `sse::load`/`sse::store`, emitting a literal `movss` via `asm!`. Both profiles now match the C on aligned, unaligned and null pointers. Read/write **order** is unchanged, which is what makes aliased pointers agree.\n\n## The most important finding was about my own harness\n\nThe crate is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` and no test target links it, so **cargo never rebuilds it during `cargo test`**. My first suite was comparing the C against a `.so` from an earlier `cargo build` — it passed with all 24 rows green while `src/lib.rs` contained a deliberately broken dispatch. I only caught this because I ran mutation testing and *every* mutant reported \"not caught\". The harness now builds the `cdylib` itself and asserts the artifact is newer than every file in `src/`.\n\nThat is also why I did not treat green tests as evidence: 30 mutants were injected, and the suite must produce the expected verdict for each.\n\n## Four mutants are provably equivalent, not coverage gaps\n\nI did not paper over these — I verified each against the C binary and documented why the C's exact form is not load-bearing:\n\n- `mulss` operand order is free (every `mul` has one constant, never-NaN operand, so the NaN tie-break can't fire).\n- `x - c*y` → `x + (-c)*y` is free — so GCC's `subss` and LLVM's preferred rewrite really are interchangeable here.\n- Swapping tritanopia's two `0.8739092…` coefficients is free: **all seven** near-duplicate literal pairs in `lib.c` round to the *same* `f32`, which is exactly why GCC emitted one shared `.rodata` entry per pair.\n\n## Coverage\n\n~830 M bit-exact comparisons per profile, including **exhaustive** sweeps over all 2²⁴ NaN encodings in each channel (604 M) and with all three channels NaN at once (75 M) — the only shape where the destination-wins tie-break is observable. Every one of the 24 float constants and all nine output expressions' operand orders were checked instruction-by-instruction against `objdump` of the C object.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: the NaN-sign fidelity depends on the C being built at `-O0`, which is what `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` produces today (it sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`). If that file ever gained `-O2`, GCC could re-order operands and rows 25–26 would be the tests that notice."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":806,"cache_read_input_tokens":206712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":806,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6f88a36-a7a8-4415-a6ac-c0933f14076b","uuid":"6cb8838f-22e4-458d-a160-1172b0990729","timestamp":"2026-08-26T21:00:38.396Z"}
